  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nHigh pressure was in place across the Northland early this \nafternoon. An area of low pressure was located over southwestern \nManitoba with a cold front trailing back into far northern Montana. \nSome low stratus was lingering across the Northland underneath the \nhigh, mainly across the Brainerd Lakes area to the Iron Range and \nareas west of the I-35 corridor. There is a narrow window for an \nisolated shower or storm this afternoon into early evening, but the \nsmoke aloft may act to keep anything from developing. Overall \nchances are around 20% or less for any rainfall today, mainly across \nnortheastern Minnesota. Any storm that does develop may briefly be \nstrong with plenty of instability to work with, but is not expected \nto last long with limited shear in place. Dry conditions then \nprevail for tonight with lows falling into the 50s and 60s, coolest \nnear Lake Superior.\n\nAnother warm and humid day is on tap for Wednesday. Highs will reach \ninto the 80s and lower 90s with dewpoints in the 60s leading to \nmuggy conditions. With widespread Moderate HeatRisk conditions \nacross the region for Wednesday along with WBGTs above 79F from late \nmorning through late afternoon, have issued a Heat Advisory for much \nof the Northland. There will again be a low chance (<20%) for an \nisolated shower or thunderstorm across most of the region tomorrow \nafternoon into early evening. We'll again see plenty of instability, \nbut lingering smoke aloft and limited shear will limit this threat. \nWe may also remained too capped for any storms to occur with no \ntrigger mechanism in place and convective temperatures in the middle \nto upper 90s.\nSome showers and storms may move in Wednesday night from the \nNorthern Plains, but confidence on this is currently low. \n\nTemperatures remain warm and quite muggy for Thursday, but there \nwill be increasing chances for showers and thunderstorms heading \ninto the afternoon hours as the upper ridge starts to shift east and \nbring a shortwave through the Upper Midwest. Strong to severe storms \nwill be possible and shear looks a little better ahead of the front \nThursday afternoon into early evening with 30 to 40 knots of \neffective shear. We are currently outlooked for a Marginal Risk by \nthe SPC and this seems appropriate at this time. While humidity will \nincrease Thursday, temperatures will be slightly cooler. Another \nround of heat headlines may be needed.\n\nRainfall chances will then linger into Friday before drying out for \nthe weekend. The jet stream will then shift north of the border for \nthe weekend and into next week, keeping the main storm track to our \nnorth. There will be limited chances for precipitation, but nothing \nsignificant at this point. Temperatures cool into the 70s and \nlow 80s for Friday and Saturday before slowly warming heading \ninto the new week.",
